### Commit 3 · HPSMPOC-289 (the part-header LINE limit; PRODUCT code; do it LAST)
**Ticket (MEASURED by Jira GET):** "BFF form counter has no part-header LINE limit: a header block of 17 or more lines under 16 KiB is counted (10) but the API refuses it on HeadersCountLimit 16 (pre-existing, harmless: the API answers 400)". Done-when: `fileByteCounter` over a 17-line header block (the Content-Disposition naming files, then 16 more header lines, under 16 KiB) + 10 B → **0** once the line limit is mirrored; red at `befd45e` (it counts 10). Fix-shape (the gate's, "optional"): "mirror HeadersCountLimit=16 in isFilePart's caller, or document it".
**Premises (MEASURED at `54fbceac`):** the counter's `"headers"` state is `formGate.ts:268-283`; the byte limit lives in `if (k > pos) { lines += k - pos; pos = seen = k + 2; if (lines >= MAX_PART_HEADERS) state = "end"; continue; }` **`:275-280`** (`lines` is the header lines' BYTES, `:224`); the empty line that ends the block is `k === pos` and calls `isFilePart(...)` `:281`; `state = "end"` makes every later chunk count 0 (`:227`, `:239`). `MAX_PART_HEADERS` `:195`. The API: `new MultipartReader(boundary, body) { HeadersCountLimit = 16, HeadersLengthLimit = 16 * 1024 }` at `api/src/HpsmPoc.Modules.Feedback/Endpoints/FeedbackFiles.cs:338` (the ticket's `:300` has drifted; every other premise of the ticket holds at main).
**Why this is the risky commit.** Giving up on a block the API reads lets that part's file bytes (and every later file part's) escape the files' total at the BFF: that is the direction B267/B271 guarded for the byte limit ("0 bypass"). So the mirrored limit may only be EQUAL to the API's or LOOSER, never tighter. **Measure before you write product code (M1, M2 below); if the measurement does not give an exact rule, STOP (see the decision rule).**
- **M1 · What does the API's `HeadersCountLimit` count, and where is the boundary?** The drafter did NOT measure this (UNMEASURED). The ticket and the gate say "a >16-line part header", but B267's sweep covered only 1 to 3 lines (MEASURED, `dnlimit-Program.cs`: shapes 1, 2, 3). **The drafter's recollection of ASP.NET Core's `MultipartReader.ReadHeadersAsync` is that it compares `HeadersCountLimit` with the number of DISTINCT header names (a case-insensitive key count in a `KeyValueAccumulator`), not with the number of lines: UNMEASURED and possibly wrong. If it is right, 17 lines that repeat one header name are READ by the API, and a plain 17-line limit at the BFF would give up on a form the API accepts: the exact bypass above.** Measure it with a copy of B267's probe (`.tools/b267/dnlimit/`, or `Briefs/2026-10-18_B267_evidence/probes/dnlimit-Program.cs`; copy under `.tools/b381/dnlimit/`, build there; one heavy job, load1 < 8; the same ASP.NET Core as the hosted API, quote its version): for n = 14..20 lines, in these families, outcome (read / threw + message) at chunkings k = 1, 7, 4096, 1 MiB: **(i)** n distinct names (`H1: x`, `H2: x`, ...) with the Content-Disposition line first; **(ii)** n lines with ONE repeated name (`X-Pad: p`); **(iii)** names differing only in letter case (`X-Pad`, `x-pad`, `X-PAD`); **(iv)** names that differ only by a trailing space before the colon (`X-Pad :`) and by a non-ASCII letter's case (`é`/`É`); **(v)** the Content-Disposition repeated n times; **(vi)** a line with no colon (the gate's "8000 one-byte lines" would be that: say what the API does). Controls: a family the byte limit refuses at 16,384 (the known boundary, `dnlimit-limit.tsv`) must still read 16,383 in your build, so the probe is the same machine as B267's; and the n = 1 case reads. Put the TSV in `Briefs/2026-10-21_B381_evidence/`. Quote what `10.0.12` and the hosted API's runtime are (RELAYED: the hosted API runs 10.0.12, B267's `limit-dn12.tsv` vs `limit-dn13.tsv` agreed on the byte boundary; re-run on whichever is installed and say which).
- **Decision rule after M1 (write the result in STATUS before any product edit):** (a) **the API counts LINES and 16 is read, 17 refused (every family)** → mirror a LINE count: give up when the block's header-line count exceeds 16. (b) **the API counts DISTINCT NAMES (case-insensitive)** → the mirror counts distinct names, with the folding rule "when two names might be one name to the API, treat them as ONE" (any fold the BFF is unsure about must err toward fewer distinct names, because fewer distinct names = the BFF gives up less = it counts more); use ASCII case-folding plus, for non-ASCII letters, `toUpperCase().toLowerCase()` on the UTF-8 decoding of the name bytes only if M1 (iv) shows .NET folds them, otherwise treat every non-ASCII-bearing name as possibly equal to an existing one and do not count it as new (state this in a comment); the Done-when's example then uses 17 DISTINCT names, and you record that the ticket's "17-line" wording was loose. (c) **anything else** (a different count, a dependency on chunking, the byte limit interacting, a runtime difference) or **M1 cannot be built or run in 20 minutes of wall-clock** → **do not write product code**; ship the FALLBACK: a comment-only change at `formGate.ts:190-195`'s doc (the gate's "or document it": state that the BFF has no header-count limit, the API's is 16 and what it counts, per M1) plus a pin test that documents the CURRENT behaviour (a 17-name block is counted: 10; so a future limit cannot be added loosely), then the last line names it in `READY FOR QA` as "-289 documented, not mirrored" and the reason goes to FOR TUESDAY. A STOP with one question is also acceptable if the measurement shows a hazard.
- **M2 · The observable difference (record it, do not pin it):** a form with an API-refused header block AND over 20 MiB of files used to get **413** from the BFF (it counted those bytes); with the limit it gets the API's 400 (`feedback.form-invalid`) unless the 21 MiB body cap fires first. No browser sends such a form. Run it once at base and once after the change through `handleApi` with the stand-in and quote both statuses; say in FOR TUESDAY that this is the one status that changes, and that no new word is involved (the 400 and the 413 bodies are the existing ones).
- **The edit if (a) or (b) (exact shape, you write the lines):** in `fileByteCounter` add the state next to `lines` `:224` (a count, or a `Set<string>` of folded names), reset it where `lines = 0` is reset `:264`; in the `if (k > pos)` branch `:275-280` account for the line that just ended (its name is the bytes from `pos` to the first `:`; a line with no colon: whatever M1 (vi) says, and never tighter than the API) and set `state = "end"` as `:278` does when the API's limit is passed. Keep `MAX_PART_HEADERS` as is. Update the doc comment `:206-213` and `:190-195` in the same hunk (comment lines: say what the limit mirrors and the M1 source). Do not touch `bff.ts`, the `export` lines or `meterForm`. The `"headers"` branch must stay linear in the bytes fed (the HPSMPOC-285 test at `formMeter.test.ts:315-375` is the instrument and must stay green): a per-line `Set` add and a name slice are O(line), not O(block); say how you kept it linear and quote that test's green result.
- **Rows (RED at base, GREEN after; each fed whole, one byte per chunk and three random chunkings, reusing the `counts()` helper of the HPSMPOC-284 describe at `formMeter.test.ts:~233-253` (copy its shape into a new describe `HPSMPOC-289` if the helper is not in scope)):** a block at the API's limit minus one and AT the limit (15 and 16 names/lines): counted (10) — the **control that keeps the limit from being tighter**; limit + 1 (17) and limit + 4: **0**; a file part AFTER a refused block is not counted either (the shape of `formMeter.test.ts:~280`); under rule (b): 17 lines over 16 distinct names, and 17 lines over names that differ only by case: counted (10); under rule (a) the same families are whatever M1 measured. Every row's expected value comes from M1's TSV (name the TSV row). A row that passes at base is a control, say so.
- **Existing tests that WILL be affected:** `formMeter.test.ts` "the memory bound: a block waiting for its end is held in at most 3 x 16 KiB (one-byte header lines, fed 1 KiB per chunk)" (MEASURED: it starts at `:285`; it builds `Array.from({ length: LIMIT - 1 - 70 }, () => "a")`, i.e. about 16,300 one-byte lines `a`, none with a colon, and expects the part counted 10 and the held block to reach more than 2 x 16 KiB). Under rule (a) that block is now given up on at the 17th line: `n` reads 0 and the control `largest > 2 * LIMIT` fails. Under rule (b) lines without a colon are not names and the test may stay green. **Either way: run it, and if it needs a change, change the SMALLEST thing (the line shape, e.g. `a:` lines repeating one name under rule (b); or the expected `n` and the worst case's definition under rule (a)), name it in STATUS with the reason ("the worst case for what is held changed because the limit now bounds the lines"), and keep a test that still measures the 3 x MAX_PART_HEADERS held-bytes bound with the new worst case.** No other existing assertion changes; if another goes red, that is a finding: STOP the commit and say which.
- **Mutants (on the fix worktree's `formGate.ts`, anchored, restored byte-equal, never committed):** **m289-a** the limit one too loose (`> 17` in place of `> 16`, or the folded equivalent): the "17 → 0" row must go red; **m289-b** one too tight (`>= 16`): the "16 → counted" control must go red; **m289-c** the count resets on every chunk (chunking dependence): a one-byte-per-chunk row must go red; **m289-d** (rule (b) only) the name compare made case-sensitive: the case-only row must go red; optionally **m289-e** the new state is not reset between parts: a two-part body (a 10-line part, then another 10-line part) must go red (add that row). Report each mutant's red tests BY NAME and any existing row it turns red.
